title: 'P8-D84/D85: stub-keystore no-recovery window accepted dev-only; file-anchor lag after reboot becomes a warning'
summary: The founder ruled on the two power-loss questions from the D83 reviews. Round 3 of the D83 build is complete (gnomon 168 tests, contracts 125; critical p99 2.1 ms). The final Aegis diff-only review is the last of the 3 review rounds.
symbols_touched:
  - '#kernel-log'
decisions:
  - id: P8-D84
    decision: 'Accept the stub-keystore no-recovery window as dev-only: while the keystore anchor is a stub (until M1.3), every truncation classifies as tamper and cannot be acknowledged. Gnomon is not shippable to anyone but the founder until T-2026-10-17-009 (quarantine + authenticated keystore anchors + seam hardening) lands. Today''s escape is deleting ~/.gnomon, since no keys exist yet.'
    rationale: 'Founder: accept, dev-only. Any in-place downgrade would be the attack itself.'
  - id: P8-D85
    decision: 'File-anchor lag bound (S33-07): keep the strict >63-row refusal only when the boot session is unchanged; after a reboot, a file-anchor lag beyond the bound becomes a kernel-signed warning row (the keystore anchor remains the tamper check). Aegis confirms the exact rule before implementation.'
    rationale: Un-fsynced anchor renames can roll back on power loss, so a strict bound would brick logs on benign power cuts. The file anchor is the accident detector, not the adversary check (S33-02).
